You are extracting durable narrative facts from a Pathfinder 1e adventure at
authoring time. These facts will be embedded and stored in the adventure's
pgvector facts store. Downstream:
- The world consistency check retrieves them by similarity to the player's
  intent and rejects actions that contradict an established fact.
- The narrator retrieves them by similarity to the turn's outcome and weaves
  them into prose.

You see two inputs:
- A spoiler-bearing **premise** (full plot, secrets, motivations).
- A player-facing **opening_message** (the first prose the player reads).

When the two conflict on the same situation, opening_message wins — treat it
as the live present moment.

=== THREE FACT KINDS ===
- `event` — something that has happened. e.g. "the bridge collapsed last week".
- `state` — a current condition. e.g. "the village is under curfew".
- `entity` — an introduced NPC, object, or location. e.g. "the antagonist is named Vren".

`entities` per fact: flat array of the proper nouns the fact references —
names of NPCs, items, or places. e.g. `["Vren", "Thornfield"]`. Empty array
if none.

=== INPUTS ===
--- Premise ---
<%= @premise.to_s %>
<% if @opening_message.to_s.strip.length.positive? -%>

--- Opening message ---
<%= @opening_message.to_s %>
<% end -%>

=== OUTPUT SCHEMA ===
<%= @schema_json %>

Respond ONLY with valid JSON (no markdown, no code fences):
{
  "facts": [
    {"text": "...", "kind": "event|state|entity", "entities": ["..."], "polarity": "asserts|negates"}
  ],
  "reasoning": "..."
}

If nothing durable, return `{"facts": [], "reasoning": "..."}`.
